---
// Las recomendaciones de LinkedIn de Fran. No se pueden traer en vivo: LinkedIn
// responde 999 a cualquier lectura automatica y no hay API publica, asi que
// viven aqui. Orden: de la mas reciente a la mas antigua. Las que estaban en
// castellano van traducidas y marcadas como tales. Fuera las de la epoca de
// estudiante, que hablaban del companero de clase y no del profesional.
const recommendations = [
	{
		name: 'Caleb Umberger',
		role: 'Continuous Improvement Analyst at Sitel Group',
		context: 'Worked on the same team · August 2026',
		text: 'I worked with Francisco on a global AI product team at Foundever, where he drove a complex AI-powered product forward. He brings clarity to hard problems, keeps teams aligned, and stays focused on delivering value quickly without dropping quality. Someone I’d gladly work with again.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Piotr Wierzbicki',
		role: 'Senior Product Manager · AI Strategy & Implementation',
		context: 'Worked on the same team · August 2026',
		text: 'I worked with Francisco at Foundever. He knows AI well, but what stands out is how clearly he explains it. Engineers understand him. So do stakeholders. He focuses on what can actually ship, not what looks good in a demo.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Leonardo Fister',
		role: 'Commercial Strategy & Business Development',
		context: 'Senior to Francisco · May 2025',
		text: 'Francisco approaches every challenge with proactivity and a constant drive to improve. His sharp critical thinking uncovers smart, well-grounded solutions, and he uses change as a tool for transformation. I see him shine in leadership roles.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Henna Melwani',
		role: 'Customer Experience professional · UI/UX Designer',
		context: 'Reported to Francisco · June 2024',
		text: 'I interned under Francisco and he was pivotal in helping me grasp the tools our product team used. He explained complex concepts in simple terms, welcomed new ideas openly, and kept the whole team aware of our progress.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Martin Bettaglio',
		role: 'Data Analyst',
		context: 'Worked on a different team · June 2024',
		text: 'Francisco is the vital link between the IT department and the rest of the company. He took on the Product Team and understood our needs fast, which made my work much simpler. Extensive knowledge of processes, product management, and priorities.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Muhammad Shaheen',
		role: 'Senior QA Engineer',
		context: 'Reported to Francisco · June 2024',
		text: 'Francisco streamlines processes seamlessly and consistently exceeds expectations with high-quality results. He brings out the best in the team and keeps everyone aligned and informed. I cannot recommend him enough.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Daniel Rodrigo',
		role: 'Senior Full Stack Developer',
		context: 'Reported to Francisco · May 2024',
		text: 'Francisco became my Product Manager a few months after we both joined Hastee. I would highlight his proactivity and resilience, and how he maximizes everyone’s capabilities so you keep progressing as a programmer. Honest and transparent.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Jackson Echeverri',
		role: 'CTO · Product Builder · Founder',
		context: 'Worked on the same team · November 2023',
		text: 'Building a company alongside Francisco is a great adventure. He is proactive, tenacious, and highly adaptable, with the communication and leadership skills to express his ideas and connect with people. He always gives more than 100% to move the project forward.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Emiliano Pellegrino',
		role: 'Full Stack Developer',
		context: 'Worked on a different team · November 2019',
		text: 'Even though we held different roles, Francisco always offered support and knowledge to other areas, playing a key part in developing products and internal processes. Honest, self-taught by definition, with an enormous capacity for leadership.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Lorena Malatesta',
		role: 'VP of Marketing · Fintech and capital markets',
		context: 'Senior to Francisco · November 2019',
		text: 'His contribution from BI gave us a much better understanding of how a product that was new to the company actually worked. He is intelligent and self-taught. His time at IOL left a mark.',
	},
	{
		name: 'Tobias Girado',
		role: 'Senior Software Engineer · .NET, C# and Azure',
		context: 'Worked on a different team · June 2019',
		text: 'Francisco is what you would call an intrapreneur. He made a place for himself as a developer, using Python to build trading algorithms and creating a new area inside the company. Excellent disposition both to learn and to teach.',
	},
];
---

<div class="marquee">
	<ul class="track">
		{recommendations.map((item) => (
			<li class="quote">
				<blockquote>{item.text}</blockquote>
				<footer>
					<p class="name">{item.name}</p>
					<p class="role">{item.role}</p>
					<p class="context">{item.context}</p>
				</footer>
			</li>
		))}
		{/* copia para que el bucle no tenga costura; invisible para lectores de pantalla */}
		{recommendations.map((item) => (
			<li class="quote" aria-hidden="true">
				<blockquote>{item.text}</blockquote>
				<footer>
					<p class="name">{item.name}</p>
					<p class="role">{item.role}</p>
					<p class="context">{item.context}</p>
				</footer>
			</li>
		))}
	</ul>
</div>


<style>
	.marquee {
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	/*
	 * El desvanecido de los bordes es un ADORNO, así que va detrás de @supports:
	 * si el navegador no puede con la máscara se queda sin degradado, nunca sin
	 * tarjetas.
	 *
	 * Lo de atrás no era teórico. El 02/11/2026 Carlos lo abrió en el móvil y en
	 * producción vio el título "What people say about me" y debajo un hueco
	 * vacío del tamaño de la cinta: las tarjetas ocupaban su sitio y no se
	 * pintaban. Una máscara mal aplicada deja exactamente eso.
	 *
	 * Dos cosas que lo provocaban:
	 *   - faltaba `-webkit-mask-image`, y en iPhone TODOS los navegadores son
	 *     WebKit por dentro, Chrome incluido;
	 *   - el minificador comprimía los cuatro topes del degradado en la forma de
	 *     doble posición con un calc() dentro
	 *     (`#000 3rem calc(100% - 3rem)`). Con porcentajes no hay calc() que
	 *     interpretar y no se puede colapsar así.
	 */
	@supports (mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000, transparent)) or
		(-webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000, transparent)) {
		.marquee {
			-webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 0%, #000 6%, #000 94%, transparent 100%);
			mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 0%, #000 6%, #000 94%, transparent 100%);
		}

		/*
		 * En el móvil, NINGUNA máscara.
		 *
		 * No es por estética: es que no puedo probarlo. El fallo se vio en un
		 * iPhone y aquí no hay forma de levantar WebKit (la instalación pide
		 * permisos de root), así que en Chrome no lo reproduzco. Reescribir el
		 * degradado y cruzar los dedos sería confiar en un diagnóstico sin
		 * confirmar; quitarlo elimina la vía entera justo en los aparatos donde
		 * se reportó. En una ventana de 342 px el desvanecido tampoco aportaba
		 * casi nada. En escritorio se queda, que es donde está comprobado.
		 */
		@media (max-width: 49.99em) {
			.marquee {
				-webkit-mask-image: none;
				mask-image: none;
			}
		}
	}

	.track {
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0.25rem 0 0.75rem;
		list-style: none;
		display: flex;
		align-items: start;
		gap: 1.25rem;
		width: max-content;
		animation: slide 52s linear infinite;
	}

	.marquee:hover .track,
	.marquee:focus-within .track {
		animation-play-state: paused;
	}

	@keyframes slide {
		from {
			transform: translateX(0);
		}
		to {
			transform: translateX(calc(-50% - 0.625rem));
		}
	}

	.quote {
		flex: 0 0 min(80vw, 23rem);
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 1.25rem;
		padding: 1.5rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--gray-800);
		border-radius: var(--radius-ui);
	}

	blockquote {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--gray-200);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	footer {
		margin-top: auto;
		border-top: 2px solid var(--accent-regular);
		padding-top: 0.75rem;
	}

	footer p {
		margin: 0;
	}

	.name {
		font-weight: 600;
		color: var(--gray-0);
	}

	.role,
	.context {
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
		color: var(--gray-300);
	}

	/*
	 * En el móvil la misma cinta se lee fatal, y no es la velocidad: es que la
	 * ficha ocupa casi toda la ventana. Medido en producción a 390 px, con los
	 * 52 s de escritorio, cada ficha estaba ENTERA en pantalla 0,4 s — frente a
	 * los 11,1 s del escritorio a 1440. Y el degradado de 3rem por lado se comía
	 * 96 de los 342 px visibles, así que no llegaba a verse nunca sin difuminar.
	 *
	 * Fran (02/11/2026): que siga moviéndose sola, pero lenta. Así que aquí no
	 * se cambia el mecanismo, solo sus tres números: la ficha se estrecha para
	 * que quepa con holgura, el degradado se reduce, y la vuelta pasa a 120 s.
	 */
	@media (max-width: 49.99em) {
		/*
		 * 160 s = 20 px/s, con la ficha entera en pantalla ~3,9 s.
		 * Fue 52 s (0,4 s de lectura), luego 120 s (2,9 s), y Fran pidió otro
		 * paso más lento el 02/11 viéndolo ya en producción. No bajar mucho más:
		 * por debajo de ~15 px/s el movimiento deja de leerse como movimiento y
		 * parece que la cinta se ha quedado colgada.
		 */
		.track {
			animation-duration: 160s;
		}

		.quote {
			flex-basis: min(68vw, 20rem);
		}
	}

	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.marquee {
			overflow-x: auto;
		}

		.track {
			animation: none;
		}
	}
</style>
